      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for that. wait, i think i misread your apa section though. for 3 or more authors in apa 7th, doesn't it just use 'et al.' right from the first citation? i thought the 'list them all' rule was mostly an older version of the manual. can you double check that for me?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, you're absolutely right. Your understanding is correct, and I stand corrected. In the APA 7th Edition, there is a streamlined approach for citing works with three or more authors:\n\n**APA 7th Edition:**\n1. Single author: (Author's Last Name, Year)\n2. Two authors: (Author's Last Name & First Initial, Year) OR (Author's Last Name, Year)\n3. Three or more authors: (Author's Last Name et al., Year). Here, 'et al.' refers to 'and others,' so you only need to list the first author followed by 'et al.' and the year in the parenthetical citation unless the source is mentioned as the final reference.\n\nFor the reference list, whenever you cite a source with three or more authors, all author names should be included in the first citation and thereafter for the sake of completeness and helpfulness to the reader. In subsequent citations within the text, 'et al.' is used to indicate the authors.\n\nI apologize for the confusion. Thank you for bringing it to my attention, and I hope this clears it up for your literature review. Remember to always check the latest edition of the APA Publication Manual or official guidelines from the American Psychological",
